199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (Timeline / History of the Sahel Crisis)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (1960: Mali, Niger, Burkina Faso, Nigeria, and other Sahelian nations become independent of colonial rule. Weak institutions, inadequate infrastructure, inadequately trained security personnel, and borders that cross ethnic and tribal communities are all inherited by new administrations. With minimal presence in rural and northern areas, state power is still concentrated in capital cities.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E <3230303185323030363A>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E ( The Sahel received more attention as part of international counter-extremism strategies following the 9/11 attacks. The United States expanded military training, intelligence sharing, and security cooperation through initiatives such as the Pan-Sahel Initiative and later the Trans-Sahara Counterterrorism Partnership \(TSCTP\), which provided security assistance to over 10 African states, including Mali, Niger, and Burkina Faso \(U.S. Department of State, 2006\). France maintained significant military and political influence due to its historical ties with former colonies. During this period, security assistance increased substantially, while governance reform, justice systems, and socioeconomic development received comparatively limited funding, allowing institutional weaknesses and public grievances to persist.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (2011:)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E ( Following the collapse of Libya, an estimated tens of thousands of weapons, including heavy arms and ammunition, flowed into the Sahel from unsecured Libyan stockpiles \(United Nations Security Council [UNSC], 2012\). Former combatants returned to Mali and Niger with advanced weapons, while national armies lacked the capacity to control borders spanning thousands of kilometers. This uncontrolled proliferation significantly strengthened non-state armed actors and increased regional instability.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
